# Release and privacy

- Inventory distributable code, dependencies, assets, schemas, tables, fixtures, notices, docs, archives, and metadata.
- Keep raw original materials, private registries, provider transcripts, source paths, identities, secrets, and unapproved evidence out of public bundles.
- Preserve applicable third-party notices/licenses and unresolved provenance findings. Confidential evidence retention does not waive legal obligations.
- Produce a redacted process/coverage report with opaque evidence IDs and bounded claims.
- Prepare a concrete local candidate before any external approval request.
- Creating, opening, submitting, publishing, or marking ready a PR to a popular/high-visibility/third-party upstream requires explicit user approval in the current conversation.
- Do not send messages or upload private records without authorization.

Example: export a sanitized technical report and independently written implementation; keep the original binary and private evidence registry owner-only.
